  <p>
    A fence looks like a simple purchase until the details start stacking up: how tall it can legally be, whether the
    city wants a permit, whether your HOA has already decided the color, and which material will still look decent after
    a few St. George summers. This guide walks through those questions in the order they actually come up, so you can
    talk to fence companies with the answers already in hand.
  </p>

  <h2>How a fence project runs, from first idea to finished fence</h2>
  <p>Most residential fence jobs follow the same sequence. Skipping an early step is how homeowners end up moving a fence line or paying for a redo.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Confirm your property lines.</strong> Use your plat, survey, or the corner pins on your lot. A fence built a few inches over a line is a neighbor dispute waiting to happen, and the installer generally builds where you point.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the city height and setback rules</strong> (next section), and note whether any part of the fence sits in a front setback.</li>
    <li><strong>Get HOA approval first, if you have an HOA.</strong> City approval and HOA approval are separate; one does not replace the other. See our <a href="/hoa-fence-requirements-st-george-ut/">HOA fence requirements guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Pull a permit if one is required.</strong> The <a href="/fence-permit-st-george-ut/">permit guide</a> explains exactly when.</li>
    <li><strong>Have utilities marked.</strong> Call 811 (<a href="https://www.bluestakes.org/" rel="noopener">Blue Stakes of Utah</a>) before any digging. The service asks for at least 3 business days of notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose a material and collect written quotes</strong> from at least two or three independent companies for the same scope.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation, then a walkthrough.</strong> Check gate swing, latch operation, post alignment, cleanup, and haul-away before the final payment.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Height and placement rules in St. George</h2>
  <p>
    St. George City Code §10-18-1 governs fence height. In plain terms, for most zones: no fence may exceed 4 feet in the
    front yard, and no fence may exceed 6 feet 4 inches in the side and rear yards. Within 10 feet behind the sidewalk,
    only decorative fences, such as picket or wrought iron styles that are at least 50 percent open, are permitted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some zones (RE-37.5, RE-5, A-1, A-5, A-10, and R-1-40) allow a fence up to 6 feet 4 inches within the front setback,
    provided it is at least 50 percent open and stays at least 10 feet from the sidewalk or property line. Barbed wire is
    prohibited in residential zones except for agricultural use in residential estates. Zoning varies by lot, so check
    which zone yours falls in before you design anything.
  </p>
  <div class="sgfw-permit">
    <strong>Permit trigger to remember:</strong> under City Code §10-18-5, a permit is required before you build any fence
    or retaining wall in a front setback, unless it is shown in detail on an approved site development plan. Height does
    not matter for that front-setback rule.
  </div>

