    for sources and method.
  </p>
  <CostTable caption={`Installed fence cost per linear foot, as of ${COST_AS_OF}`} unit="per linear foot" rows={perFoot} />

  <h3>Wood privacy fences</h3>
  <p>
    Wood is the classic backyard choice and among the most affordable privacy options. In a hot, dry climate, unfinished
    boards dry out, and a fence that is never sealed or stained will gray, warp, and split sooner. Ask how the company sets
    posts and whether the boards are treated, then plan for periodic re-sealing as part of the true cost of ownership.
  </p>

  <h3>Vinyl (PVC) fences</h3>
  <p>
    Vinyl needs almost no painting or sealing and comes in white, tan, and wood-grain finishes. The questions worth asking
    in a sunny climate are about the formulation: whether the panels are UV-stabilized, how the manufacturer handles
    fading or brittleness in its warranty, and whether the posts are reinforced. Long runs also need room for thermal
    movement, so how the company connects panels matters.
  </p>

  <h3>Chain-link</h3>
  <p>
    Chain-link is the price leader and works well for containing pets and marking a boundary, especially on larger lots. It
    offers little privacy on its own. Vinyl-coated or powder-coated versions cost more than plain galvanized but hold their
    appearance better. Slats can add privacy, though they also catch wind.
  </p>

  <h3>Ornamental iron and aluminum</h3>
  <p>
    Open-picket metal fences are durable, look sharp, and fit the 50-percent-open rule that applies to decorative fences
    near the sidewalk in the front yard. They provide little privacy, and steel needs a good finish to resist rust; ask
    what coating the company uses. Aluminum avoids rust but is generally lighter.
  </p>

  <h3>Block and masonry</h3>
  <p>
    A block wall is a different kind of project with its own permit and cost profile. If privacy, sound, and permanence
    matter more than price, read our <a href="/block-wall-installation-st-george-ut/">block wall installation guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Wind, soil, and slope</h2>
  <p>
    Wind is a real design load for any solid privacy fence, because it acts like a sail. Post depth, spacing, and how the posts are
    set (and whether concrete is used) matter more here than the color of the panels. Ask the company how they set posts in
    your specific soil. Rocky or hard-packed ground can add labor, which shows up in the quote. On sloped lots, ask how
    the fence will step or rack down the grade, because each approach changes the look and the price.
  </p>

  <h2>Gates</h2>
  <p>
    Plan gate locations before quotes, since gates cost more per foot than fence and are the part most likely to fail.
    Confirm gate width for a mower or trailer, the hardware brand and finish, and whether the gate frame is reinforced
    against sagging. If a gate borders a pool area, read our <a href="/pool-fence-and-gate-st-george-ut/">pool fence and gate guide</a> first.
  </p>

  <h2>What drives the price</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Material.</strong> Chain-link is at the low end and ornamental iron or vinyl privacy at the high end.</li>
    <li><strong>Total length and height.</strong> Cost scales with linear feet; a taller fence uses more material and heavier posts.</li>
    <li><strong>Gates.</strong> Each gate adds hardware and labor beyond the per-foot rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Lot conditions.</strong> Slope, rock, tight access, and utility conflicts increase labor.</li>
    <li><strong>Removal of an old fence.</strong> Demolition and haul-away are often quoted separately.</li>
    <li><strong>Permits and HOA submittals.</strong> Ask who pulls the permit and whether that fee is in the quote.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Vetting checklist before you sign</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Look the company up in the <a href="https://secure.utah.gov/llv/search/index.html" rel="noopener">Utah Division of Professional Licensing license lookup</a> and confirm its license status yourself (<a href="/licensed-fence-contractor-compare-st-george-ut/">how to read the record</a>).</li>
    <li>Ask for a certificate of insurance and confirm it directly with the insurer or agent.</li>
    <li>Get a written scope: material, height, linear feet, gates, post spacing, concrete, removal, cleanup.</li>
    <li>Ask who pulls the permit and who handles HOA paperwork.</li>
    <li>Compare at least two or three quotes on the same scope. A big gap usually means the scopes differ, not that one company is a bargain.</li>
    <li>Get warranty terms in writing, including what is covered for workmanship and what the manufacturer covers.</li>
    <li>Agree on a payment schedule tied to progress; avoid paying the full amount up front.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Red flags</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pressure to sign the same day, or a "today only" price.</li>
    <li>No written estimate, or a quote that will not itemize gates, removal, and permits.</li>
    <li>A company that says permits or HOA approval are "not needed" without checking your lot.</li>
    <li>A request for a large cash deposit, or for payment in full before work starts.</li>
    <li>No verifiable license or insurance when you ask.</li>
  </ul>

